  <h1>The Echoes Return</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Margaret put the notebook in a drawer. She didn't show it to anyone. She didn't add to it.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>But she didn't throw it away.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The notebook was found in 2003, during the renovation of the diner. The diner had been closed for a decade — Margaret had died in 1995, and no one had wanted to buy the business, and the building had sat empty, waiting for someone to decide what to do with it. The new owners were a young couple from out of town, and they were turning the diner into an art gallery, and they found the notebook in a drawer that had been stuck and then forced open.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>They opened it. They read it. They didn't know what to make of it.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The names meant nothing to them. The locations meant nothing. The dates were from before they were born. The notebook was a relic from a time and a place that had ceased to exist, a record of a town that had decided to forget and had almost succeeded.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>But there was something about the notebook — the care with which it had been kept, the precision of the handwriting, the weight of the thing — that made them show it to someone. They showed it to the librarian, a woman named Ellen who had been born in Harmon Falls and who was the kind of person who knew things about the town that the town didn't know about itself, or had decided not to know.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Ellen looked at the notebook for a long time. She read every name. She walked to the river — the river was still there, had always been there, would always be there — and she found some of the locations, the places where the names had been carved and had waited and had come back.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>"This should go to the historical society," she said.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>It did. It was scanned, digitized, put in a folder with other documents from the period. It was not displayed. It was not made into a display. It was simply there, in the archive, waiting.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>The river runs the same path it has always run. The valley catches the morning light and holds it until noon. The names that were carved into the stones and the trees and the wooden posts have almost all disappeared — worn away by weather and time and the deliberate forgetting of a community that decided it couldn't carry the weight of its own history. But not quite all. Some are still there, waiting, holding their small records against the silence.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>Someone will find them. Someone always does.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>And as long as someone finds them, the thing that happened here — the war that came home, the losses that didn't fit the stories, the echoes that never stopped echoing — will not be entirely forgotten. It will be there, in the names, in the places, in the record that someone made when no one else would.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p>It will be there, waiting to return.</p>
<div class="scene-break" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
